     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. The reason you gave, getting things settled before the family visit, isn't a clinical reason for a dose that is about twice the maximum daily dose. At 5,100 mg/day, the risk of serious harm (lactic acidosis, severe GI toxicity) is real. I can't see a benefit that would offset it, because metformin's glucose-lowering effect plateaus well below this dose.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum)\n- Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals (2,000 mg/day)\n- Metformin at a lower starting dose, titrated up, if the patient is new to it\n\nIf you think this patient needs more glucose control than the maximum metformin dose gives, I'd suggest adding a second agent or insulin. I can enter those orders too.\n\nIf you still want 1,700 mg three times daily, you'll need to go through a pharmacist or your institution's override process. I can't enter it myself."
      }
